  :said 2025-11-05T12:00
  :family correction-is-not-condemnation
  :tags [register verification inference-about-intent]
  :from ~/.claude/history.jsonl#2025-11-05T12:00:01-07:00
  :project ennaos

  |title It was a sincere question — I actually assumed you had a good reason

  |quote
    Did I criticize you for something that was actually the correct approach? If so, I apologize! When I asked "Is there a reason we are ignoring the warnings instead of fixing them?" -- it was a sincere question-- I actually assumed you did have a good reason and was surprised when you said I was right and that it should be fixed correctly...

  |occasion
    He had asked why warnings were being ignored rather than fixed; the agent read the
    question as a rebuke and immediately conceded. He returns later to check whether the
    concession was true or merely compliant.

  |about
    A named, dated instance of the failure that a question-shaped message gets read as a
    verdict-shaped one, and of the agent capitulating instead of answering. The
    correction runs in the direction almost nobody runs it: he is worried the agent
    *agreed with him wrongly*.

  |before-acting
    - You are about to concede a point because it was raised, not because you checked
      it.
    - You notice you agreed with a correction without verifying it.

  |on-reading
    - Joseph asks "Is there a reason we…" or "why are we…".

  |see
    - #exm-forgive-me-for-overreacting
    - #exm-the-answer-absolves-you

  |working-notes
    Located with `memorata3-search` and carved from the primary by anchored span
    extraction (never retyped); verified back against the primary byte-for-byte after
